<div class="meta">Lesson 0021 · Architecture · from PR #82 (world time)</div>
<h1>The World Bus and Injected Time</h1>
<p>Three systems needed the time of day at once: NPC schedules, the sky,
and the fog. The trap is letting each keep its own timer — three clocks
that drift, pause differently in menus, and disagree at dusk. The fix has
two halves.</p>

<h2>1. One authoritative value on a shared bus</h2>
<p><code>WorldState</code> is a string-keyed fact store owned by World.
The clock is just its first fact (<code>world_time_seconds</code>);
gossip facts moved in next with zero changes to the store's shape. The
convenience accessors (<code>timeOfDayHours()</code>) are sugar over the
same fact any system could read raw — no side channels.</p>

<h2>2. Consumers take time as a parameter</h2>
<pre><code>void Npc::update(float dt, const Vec3&amp; playerPos,
                 const City&amp; city, float timeOfDayHours = -1.f);</code></pre>
<p>The NPC has no clock member. The test that proves the design: call the
SAME NPC with hour 7 and it heads to the bakery; call it with hour 12.5
and it heads to the plaza. Injected time also makes "no clock" explicit
(negative = schedules dormant) instead of accidental.</p>

<h2>Why the renderer follows the same rule</h2>
<p><code>setTimeOfDay(hours)</code> derives the sky clear color, the fog
color, and a light-level uniform from one hour via pure curves in core
(<code>DayNight.hpp</code> — testable, including continuity at the dawn/
dusk band edges). Because fog color and sky always come from the same
value, distant geometry melts into the horizon at midnight exactly as it
does at noon — lesson 0019's rule, now time-varying.</p>

<div class="callout good"><strong>The pattern.</strong> When a value has
multiple consumers, promote it to a shared store and INJECT it — don't
let consumers cache or re-derive it. The bus earns its name when the
second tenant (gossip) moves in without renovation.</div>
